    correct: 1,
    explain: "The PIM distinction is the point: an <b>eligible</b> assignment is not in the user's token and gives them nothing until they activate, but it is a permission that exists and that the holder can exercise on their own. For governance and audit, eligible counts: the universe of people who can be Security Administrator is fourteen, not three. Approval on activation is a configurable option, not the default, so it cannot be assumed. And PIM does govern roles assigned through role-assignable groups, so that inheritance is not invisible. <a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/id-governance/access-reviews-overview\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Microsoft Learn ↗</a>",
